---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`Privacy promise — ${SITE.name}`}
  description="How tooldo handles your files: it doesn't. Every tool runs in your browser, and nothing is uploaded, stored, or tracked."
>
  <article class="page">
    <div class="shell page__inner">
      <p class="eyebrow">the promise</p>
      <h1>{SITE.promise}</h1>

      <p class="page__lead">
        Not as a policy we could quietly change — as a fact about how the site is built.
        There is no server that could receive your file.
      </p>

      <h2>How it works</h2>
      <p>
        Every tool runs as code inside your browser tab, using WebAssembly and the same
        APIs any web page has. When you drop in a PDF or a photo, it's read from your
        disk into your own device's memory, worked on there, and handed back to you.
        Close the tab and it's gone.
      </p>

      <h2>What we collect</h2>
      <p>
        A page view, and nothing about you or your files. There's still no account and no
        error tracking. The one thing that changed: we can now see which pages get
        visited and roughly how often, through <a
          href="https://www.cloudflare.com/web-analytics/">Cloudflare Web Analytics</a> —
        no cookies, no fingerprinting, nothing that follows you to another site. It counts
        a visit to a page; it has no way to see what you drop into a tool, what you type,
        or what you download.
      </p>

      <h2>What stays on your device</h2>
      <p>
        Some tools remember your own settings so you don't retype them — invoice details,
        for instance. That lives in your browser's local storage, on your machine, and
        clearing your browser data removes it. It is never sent anywhere.
      </p>

      <h2>Checking for yourself</h2>
      <p>
        You don't have to take our word for it. Open your browser's network panel and
        use any tool: you'll see the page and its code load, and nothing leave. The
        source is public, so you can also read exactly what runs.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page {
    padding-block: var(--space-2xl) var(--space-3xl);
  }

  .page__inner {
    max-width: var(--width-prose);
  }

  .page__inner h1 {
    margin-top: var(--space-xs);
    font-size: var(--text-3xl);
    font-weight: var(--weight-bold);
  }

  .page__inner h2 {
    margin-top: var(--space-xl);
    font-size: var(--text-xl);
  }

  .page__lead {
    margin-top: var(--space-md);
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    color: var(--color-ink-soft);
  }

  .page__inner p + p,
  .page__inner h2 + p {
    margin-top: var(--space-sm);
  }
</style>
